#Part 3: Adjudicate
The article argues that there has been an increase in people living below the poverty level and a widening of wage inequality in Philadelphia between 2006-2010 and 2013-2017. Despite this being true for the estimated values, the article does not take into consideration the statistical uncertainty of the data.

Out of the 306 Census Tracts (I excluded the tracts that were NA), only 69 (23%) of them experienced a change in poverty rates that are statistically distinguishable from zero at the 90 percent confidence level. The increases and decreases in the remaining tracts cannot be used because of the uncertainty. This is also true for the change in medium household income, where out of the 306 Census Tracts, only 78 (25%) are statistically distinguishable from zero at the 90 percent confidence level. Rather that indicating that there has been a change in poverty in Philadelphia between these two time periods, the data simple shows that poverty continues to exist.

#Part 4: Rewrite

A surface-level analysis of the change in poverty level and medium household income in Philadelphia from data taken from the 5-year ACS for 2006-2010 and 2013-2017 indicate that there has been a drastic increase in poverty. However, this data does not take into consideration the margin of error of this data, and therefore the margin of error in the rate of change. 
That is, only 23% of the poverty data and 25% of the medium household income data show changes that would be considered statistically significant.
Despite this uncertainty, the data still indicates that poverty has been and continues to be pervasive in Philadelphia. There has just not been a large signifigant change.
